    "case_id": "666",
    "case_summary": "A 78-year-old female noticed a pronounced flexion of her head and neck three months before her initial hospital visit, with subsequent deterioration of symptoms. Medical history includes hypertension and dementia with no recent changes in daily medication. The patient complained of neck pain, difficulty with forward gaze, and gait disturbance. No dysphagia was present. The patient denied any neurological deficit such as motor weakness or loss of sensation in bilateral upper and lower extremities. No neuromuscular or autoimmune diseases were noted.\n\nThe patient demonstrated pronounced head and neck flexion, particularly noticeable in the standing position. Physical examination revealed cervical spine deformity with scoliotic and kyphotic changes.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    Image Title: Initial Radiographs    Image Description: A-P view and lateral views (neutral, flexion, extension, and standing) demonstrating scoliotic and kyphotic changes in cervical spine        - Laboratory Tests:    Serum electrolyte levels were within normal range during follow-up",
